window.docContents[162].resolve({"/Distance/#section-moore-graphs":{"contents":"A graph attaining the Moore bound is called a Moore graph. Equality forces every\nlayer to have the largest possible size, which (one can show) makes a Moore graph of\ndiameter k with d ≥ 1 a (d + 1)-regular graph of girth 2 * k + 1, so that it\nattains the cage bound as well. (For d = 0 the single edge K[2] attains the Moore\nbound but has no cycles.)\n\nThe simplest examples are the complete graphs: for d ≥ 1, K[d + 2] is\n(d + 1)-regular, has diameter 1 and girth 3, and both bounds with k = 1\nevaluate to d + 2. Next come the odd cycles: for k ≥ 1, C[2 * k + 1] is\n2-regular (so d = 1), has diameter k (ediam_cycleGraph) and girth 2 * k + 1\n(egirth_cycleGraph), and both bounds evaluate to 2 * k + 1, as the following\ncomputation checks.\n\nexample (k : ℕ) : 1 + (1 + 1) * ∑ i ∈ Finset.range k, 1 ^ i = 2 * k + 1 := by\n  simp [add_comm]\n\n\nAnother Moore graph is the Petersen graph, shown in the figure at the start of this\nsection. It has 10 = 1 + 3 * (1 + 2) vertices, is 3-regular, and has diameter\n2 and girth 5, so it attains both bounds with d = 2 and k = 2. We prove this\nin Moore Graphs of Diameter Two,\nwhere the theory of strongly regular graphs allows us to give short proofs.\n\nMoore graphs are rare. Besides C[5] and the Petersen graph, the only known Moore\ngraph of diameter 2 is the Hoffman–Singleton graph, which is 7-regular with 50\nvertices. The Hoffman–Singleton theorem A. J. Hoffman and R. R. Singleton, On Moore graphs with diameters 2 and 3, IBM J. Res. Develop. 4 (1960), no. 5, 497–504.\n(Strongly Regular Graphs) shows that any other example would be\n57-regular, and whether one exists is a famous open problem. For diameter at least\n3, it is known that the only Moore graphs are the odd cycles.\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Distance\u0009Moore and Cage Bounds*","header":"11.5.7. Moore Graphs","id":"/Distance/#section-moore-graphs"},"/Maps/#section-vertex-transitivity-of-hypercubes":{"contents":"The hypercube is vertex-transitive: \"translation\" by a fixed vertex t, i.e.\ncomponentwise xor with t, is an automorphism of Q[n], and translating by\nu.xor v sends u to v.\n\nComponentwise xor (symmetric difference) of two hypercube vertices./-- Componentwise xor (symmetric difference) of two hypercube vertices. -/\ndef HypercubeVert.xor : {n : ℕ} → HypercubeVert n → HypercubeVert n → HypercubeVert n\n  | 0,     _,      _      => ()\n  | _ + 1, (v, a), (w, b) => (v.xor w, a ^^ b)\n\n\nNotation v ⊕ₕ w for the componentwise xor of hypercube vertices\nv w : HypercubeVert n, i.e. addition in (ℤ/2)ⁿ. (We use ⊕ₕ rather than ⊕ to\navoid clashing with the Sum notation α ⊕ β.)\n\n@[inherit_doc HypercubeVert.xor]\nscoped infixl:65 \" ⊕ₕ \" => HypercubeVert.xor\n\n\nxor-ing twice by the same vertex is the identity, so translation is an\ninvolution.\n\n@[simp]\nlemma HypercubeVert.xor_xor {n : ℕ} (v t : HypercubeVert n) : (v ⊕ₕ t) ⊕ₕ t = v := by\n  induction n with\n  | zero => rfl\n  | succ n ih =>\n    obtain ⟨v, a⟩ := v; obtain ⟨t, s⟩ := t\n    exact Prod.ext (ih v t) (by cases a <;> cases s <;> rfl)\n\n\nWe record this using Mathlib's Function.Involutive, which gives us injectivity\nand the inverse equivalence for free.\n\nlemma HypercubeVert.xor_involutive {n : ℕ} (t : HypercubeVert n) :\n    Function.Involutive (· ⊕ₕ t) :=\n  (HypercubeVert.xor_xor · t)\n\n\nxor-ing u into u ⊕ₕ v recovers v.\n\n@[simp]\nlemma HypercubeVert.xor_xor_cancel {n : ℕ} (u v : HypercubeVert n) :\n    u ⊕ₕ (u ⊕ₕ v) = v := by\n  induction n with\n  | zero => rfl\n  | succ n ih =>\n    obtain ⟨u, a⟩ := u\n    obtain ⟨v, b⟩ := v\n    exact Prod.ext (ih u v) (by cases a <;> cases b <;> rfl)\n\n\nTranslation by t is injective.\n\n@[simp]\nlemma HypercubeVert.xor_left_inj {n : ℕ} {v w t : HypercubeVert n} :\n    v ⊕ₕ t = w ⊕ₕ t ↔ v = w :=\n  (HypercubeVert.xor_involutive t).injective.eq_iff\n\n\nTranslating both vertices of a potential edge by t preserves adjacency in\nQ[n].\n\nlemma HypercubeVert.xor_adj {n : ℕ} (v w t : HypercubeVert n) :\n    Q[n].Adj (v ⊕ₕ t) (w ⊕ₕ t) ↔ Q[n].Adj v w := by\n  induction n with\n  | zero => simp\n  | succ n ih =>\n    cases v; cases w; cases t\n    simp [ih, HypercubeVert.xor]\n\n\nTranslation of Q[n] by a fixed vertex t, via componentwise xor, as a graph\nautomorphism./-- Translation of Q[n] by a fixed vertex t, via componentwise xor, as a graph\nautomorphism. -/\ndef hypercube.translate {n : ℕ} (t : HypercubeVert n) : Q[n] ≃g Q[n] where\n  toEquiv := (HypercubeVert.xor_involutive t).toPerm\n  map_rel_iff' := HypercubeVert.xor_adj _ _ t\n\n\nThe hypercube Q[n] is vertex-transitive.\n\nlemma isVertexTransitive_hypercube (n : ℕ) : Q[n].IsVertexTransitive :=\n  fun u v => ⟨hypercube.translate (u ⊕ₕ v), HypercubeVert.xor_xor_cancel u v⟩\n\n\nWe do not describe the full automorphism group of Q[n] here: it is the wreath\nproduct ℤ/2 ≀ Sₙ, generated by the translations above together with permutations of\nthe coordinates. While our construction of the hypercube using the vertices\nHypercubeVert n is very convenient for inductive proofs, to talk about the\n\"coordinates\" of a vertex it is better to use Fin n → Bool. We return to this in\nThe Hypercube and the Hamming Cube, where we give a graph\nisomorphism Q[n] ≃g hypercubeHamming n between our box product model of the\nhypercube and the Hamming cube on Fin n → Bool, and then use it to identify the\nautomorphism group of Q[n].\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Maps\u0009Automorphisms","header":"5.6.2. Vertex-Transitivity of Hypercubes","id":"/Maps/#section-vertex-transitivity-of-hypercubes"}});